<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irrawaddy River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayeyarwady River, Ayeyarwady</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Irrawaddy, or Ayeyarwady, is the principal river of Myanmar, flowing about 2,170 kilometres from the mountains of the far north to a broad delta on the Andaman Sea. It is the country's main artery of trade and transport, and its dry-zone valley and delta supported the Pyu city-states, Pagan, Ava and later Burmese kingdoms, as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> economy of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river.jpg" alt="Irrawaddy River" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørn Christian Tørrissen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irrawaddy-River-Myanmar-Burma-2005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
